{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"first isomorphism theorem for rings first isomorphism theorem for rings","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","fileName":"First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 19:40\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Ring and Ideal Foundations]] · [[Quotient Rings and Ideals]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Ring and Ideal Foundations","Quotient Rings and Ideals","Ring Homomorphism","Quotient Ring","commutativealgebra.pdf","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"first isomorphism theorem for rings first isomorphism theorem for rings","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","fileName":"First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings","heading":"First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"For a [[Ring Homomorphism]] f: R → S, the image of f is isomorphic to the [[Quotient Ring]] R/ker(f). Elements of R that differ by an element of the kernel have the same image, and no further identifications occur.\n\nThe theorem converts a mapping problem into an ideal-and-quotient problem. In particular, every surjective ring homomorphism presents its target as the source divided by its kernel.\n\nThe text presents this as the ring analogue of the group theorem, often abbreviated FHT. When the homomorphism is onto, the target is isomorphic to the source divided by the exact ideal of elements sent to zero.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Ring and Ideal Foundations","Quotient Rings and Ideals","Ring Homomorphism","Quotient Ring","commutativealgebra.pdf","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"first isomorphism theorem for rings first isomorphism theorem for rings","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","fileName":"First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"First Isomorphism Theorem for Rings","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[commutativealgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Ring and Ideal Foundations","Quotient Rings and Ideals","Ring Homomorphism","Quotient Ring","commutativealgebra.pdf","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}